Summary: Report the user's registers for a thread suspended on its way out of a syscall
Category: system
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
From: dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c (usr1_handler); written for
 wine-mac-builds.
Source: none published
Upstream: no
Why: SuspendThread reaches the thread as SIGUSR1, and usr1_handler saves
 the context that GetThreadContext later returns. If the thread is in a
 syscall it saves the user's registers from the syscall frame; otherwise it
 takes the signal context as user code. It decides with is_inside_syscall,
 which only covers the kernel stack up to the frame. On its way out of a
 syscall, though, __wine_syscall_dispatcher points rsp into the frame
 (frame+0x70, to load the user's rsp or for iretq), and on macOS it then
 restores the user's gs base with a system call (0x3000003) from there,
 which under Rosetta is slow. A suspend landing there saved the dispatcher's
 own registers as the thread's context: rsp on Wine's kernel stack, just
 below the thread's stack, and rip in the dispatcher. Now a thread whose sp
 is in the frame is treated as in the syscall too: its context is built
 below the frame, not over it, and leave_handler then runs as it did
 before, since the dispatcher may already have switched to the user's gs
 base. Other users of is_inside_syscall are unchanged.
Programs: Steam's client (64-bit, its Chromium/CEF), since the client update
 of 2026-11-21: Chromium's stack sampling profiler suspends a thread,
 copies its stack from the reported rsp up to the stack base, and faulted
 at the thread stack's guard page (libcef.dll+0x922cc1a); the game
 started from Steam quit with it
Uncertain: Linux has the same window (frame+0x70 before iretq) without the
 extra system call, so there it should be rare; not tested there.
Evidence: Two minidumps (both 11.22-build0): the faulting loop is
 Chromium's CopyStackContentsAndRewritePointers, copying from 0x410 below a
 thread's stack reservation (the dispatcher's kernel rsp) to its stack
 base, and it faults on reaching the reservation's first page. A probe that
 suspends a thread making syscalls and reads its context saw rsp at that
 same place, with rip at __wine_syscall_dispatcher+432 (the gs-base
 syscall), in 86135 of 287461 contexts on plain wine 11.22 and in 81608 of
 253963 on 11.22-build0. 32-bit (WoW64) threads aren't affected: 0 of
 234757.
Applies to: wine 11.22 (macfixes, after 0067)
Status: 2026-11-21 (evening): Second Chance's full serial integration run
 on the same swapped tree (build0 + this ntdll.so), 09:35-14:43, passed
 every source: disc, download, Big Fish trials (all 30), Steam and Big
 Fish installs; no Steam crashes. Still not in a build.
 2026-11-21 (later): confirmed with Steam by Second Chance on meeka,
 with the same swapped tree: 0 crashes in 28 Steam starts (Blue Moon
 Canyon x3 and the four games whose launches crashed on build0), against
 7 in about 35 on 11.22-build0 the same night. Still not in a build.
 2026-11-21: not yet in a build. Tested by swapping a ntdll.so built
 with it into 11.22-build0 on meeka (macOS 26.7.2): the regression test
 suspend-context-syscall-return passes (163987 contexts, none outside the
 stack; a longer probe: 0 of 315133); on build0 it fails (49579 of 163306).
 Not yet tested with Steam, and the full suite hasn't run on that tree.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -2831,6 +2831,25 @@
 
     if (is_inside_syscall( data, RSP_sig(sigcontext) )) abort_thread( 0 );
     user_mode_abort_thread( 0, get_syscall_frame( data ));
+}
+
+
+/**********************************************************************
+ *		is_inside_syscall_frame
+ *
+ * On the way out of a syscall, the dispatcher points rsp into the syscall
+ * frame (to load the user's rsp, or for iretq) and, on macOS, restores the
+ * user's gs base with a system call while it's there. The thread is still
+ * in the syscall, but is_inside_syscall() only covers the kernel stack below
+ * the frame.
+ */
+static BOOL is_inside_syscall_frame( struct thread_data *data, ULONG_PTR sp )
+{
+    struct syscall_frame *frame;
+
+    if (!data->teb || !(frame = get_syscall_frame( data ))) return FALSE;
+    return (char *)sp > (char *)frame &&
+           (char *)sp < (char *)get_kernel_stack( data ) + kernel_stack_size;
 }
 
 
@@ -2848,14 +2867,17 @@
     {
         server_select( NULL, 0, SELECT_INTERRUPTIBLE, 0, NULL, NULL );
     }
-    else if (is_inside_syscall( data, RSP_sig(sigcontext) ))
+    else if (is_inside_syscall( data, RSP_sig(sigcontext) ) ||
+             is_inside_syscall_frame( data, RSP_sig(sigcontext) ))
     {
         struct syscall_frame *frame = get_syscall_frame( data );
+        BOOL in_frame = is_inside_syscall_frame( data, RSP_sig(sigcontext) );
+        ULONG_PTR sp = in_frame ? (ULONG_PTR)frame : RSP_sig(sigcontext);
         ULONG64 saved_compaction = 0;
         I386_CONTEXT *wow_context;
         struct xcontext *context;
 
-        context = (struct xcontext *)(((ULONG_PTR)RSP_sig(sigcontext) - 128 /* red zone */ - sizeof(*context)) & ~15);
+        context = (struct xcontext *)((sp - 128 /* red zone */ - sizeof(*context)) & ~15);
         if ((char *)context < (char *)get_kernel_stack( data ))
         {
             ERR_(seh)( "kernel stack overflow.\n" );
@@ -2892,6 +2914,8 @@
             cpu->Flags |= WOW64_CPURESERVED_FLAG_RESET_STATE;
         }
         NtSetContextThread( GetCurrentThread(), &context->c );
+        /* The dispatcher may already have switched to the user's gs base. */
+        if (in_frame) leave_handler( data, sigcontext );
     }
     else
     {
